WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.312
SQL is written in one order but it helps to
understand the

00:00:03.338 --> 00:00:07.325
conceptual order in which the parts of a query are
resolved A

00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:11.875
simplified mental model is FROM then WHERE then
SELECT then DISTINCT then

00:00:11.888 --> 00:00:17.425
ORDER BY and finally LIMIT FROM comes first
conceptually because PostgreSQL needs

00:00:17.438 --> 00:00:21.062
to know the source of the data WHERE then filters
the source

00:00:21.088 --> 00:00:25.638
rows SELECT determines the columns or expressions
that will appear in the

00:00:25.650 --> 00:00:31.425
result DISTINCT can remove repeated result rows
ORDER BY sorts

00:00:31.450 --> 00:00:37.000
the result LIMIT keeps only the requested number of
rows Why should

00:00:37.025 --> 00:00:41.400
a beginner care about this Because it explains some
behavior that can

00:00:41.425 --> 00:00:46.675
otherwise feel confusing For example WHERE filters
rows before the final sorting

00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:51.212
happens It also explains why a column alias created
in SELECT may

00:00:51.225 --> 00:00:54.462
not always be available in an earlier logical part
of the query

00:00:55.338 --> 00:00:59.950
You do not need to memorize a full database
execution engine Think

00:00:59.962 --> 00:01:03.950
of this as a reasoning tool When a query behaves
differently than

00:01:03.975 --> 00:01:08.875
you expected ask what data exists at each conceptual
stage For now

00:01:09.075 --> 00:01:12.638
remember the basic flow choose the source filter the
rows build the

00:01:12.650 --> 00:01:16.412
result remove duplicates if needed sort it and then
limit the number

00:01:16.438 --> 00:01:17.000
of rows
